    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Testicular germ cell tumours (GCTs) are the most common malignancy in men aged 15–35. They are classified as seminomas (~55%) and non-seminomatous GCTs (~45%, including embryonal carcinoma, teratoma, choriocarcinoma, and mixed). The classic presentation is a painless, hard, firm testicular mass or scrotal heaviness, often discovered incidentally by the patient. A dull ache may be present. Metastatic disease may cause back pain (retroperitoneal nodes), respiratory symptoms (pulmonary metastases), or gynaecomastia (hCG-secreting tumours). Diagnosis is by urgent scrotal ultrasound and tumour markers (AFP, hCG, LDH); orchidectomy is the definitive diagnostic and primary therapeutic procedure. Testicular GCTs are highly curable even at advanced stage.",
